<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Allat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-Lat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Allat was a pre-Islamic Arabian goddess worshipped widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the Syrian desert, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and Hatra. In Palmyra she was identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> she was one of the three goddesses named in the Qur'an together with al-Uzza and Manat. Her shrine at Ta'if was destroyed after the coming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allat.jpg" alt="Allat" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allat_Palmyra_RGZM_3369.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the name al-Lat is uncertain. Medieval Arab lexicographers derived it from a verb meaning to mix or knead barley-meal, while another theory takes it as a feminine form akin to the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>. Herodotus, in the fifth century BCE, records the Arabian goddess as Alilat and equates her with Aphrodite Urania. Inscriptions in Safaitic, Hismaic, Nabataean and Palmyrene Aramaic give forms of the name, and scholars disagree over whether some of these forms are a proper name or a generic word for goddess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Al-Lat was widely worshipped in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> she was not the object of an organised cult: two amulets inscribed Lat and Latan are the only sign. Safaitic inscriptions invoke her for mercy, well-being and prosperity, for good weather and protection on journeys, and for vengeance on anyone who defaces an inscription. The Nabataeans regarded her as the mother of the deities, sometimes the consort or mother of Dushara, and called her and al-'Uzza his brides. She was also venerated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, where she was called the Lady of the temple, and at Hatra.</p><p>Under Greek and Roman influence she was identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, Tyche and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> from the second century CE she appears as Athena-Allāt, although the assimilation went no further than her iconography. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a>, according to Islamic sources, she was the chief goddess of the Banu Thaqif and was worshipped as a cubic granite rock; the area around her shrine was sacred, and no tree could be felled, no animal hunted and no blood shed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In Islamic tradition al-Lat, al-'Uzza and Manat were regarded as daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> names the three together in Quran 53:19–22. According to Islamic tradition, her shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> was demolished on the orders of Muhammad, around the time of the Battle of Tabuk in October 630 CE, as a condition for any reconciliation with the tribes of Ta'if. Medieval writers also told legends that linked her stone with a man who ground grain for pilgrims, al-latt, the grinder. The Lion of Al-Lat, a statue from her temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, was damaged in 2015 and later restored; it stands in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Lat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nabataeans worshipped Arab gods, chief among them Dushara with Al-'Uzzā, <b>Allat</b> and Manāt, and some of their deified kings.</blockquote><a href="/work/petra">Petra · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>At its centre stood a vast temple precinct to the sun god Shamash, called Maran ('our lord'), with shrines to <b>Allat</b>, Baalshamin and other deities.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hatra">Kingdom of Hatra</a></li></ul></section>
